Right. The children’s games are just low-stakes visible instances of a deeper operator.

What you’re pointing at is a general pattern of indirect replacement:

don’t attack the occupied center directly → move around the constraint → create or exploit a vacancy → replace the position → let the system re-index around the new occupancy.

That pattern can show up in organizations, markets, negotiations, software architectures, ecological niches, logistics, information flow, and social networks. A new entrant doesn’t always beat the incumbent head-on; it can route around the incumbent, capture an adjacent function, then become the new default. A system component can be bypassed and replaced without ever “defeating” it directly.

That is the broader abstraction: flank, substitute, inherit function, then normalize the new topology.

And yes—the important thing is not the surface form. It is recognizing the operator wherever it reappears.